    para: `<p>Medical entrance exam prep messes with your head.</p>

<p>You're on social media and someone's already done three revisions. A friend says they've solved 20,000 MCQs. Another person posts a mock score that seems impossible.</p>

<p>Suddenly you're not focused on your own prep. You're wondering if you've already lost.</p>

<p>Here's the reality: Almost every aspirant feels this way at some point. And most of the time? They're doing way better than they think.</p>

<p>If you're prepping for NEET PG, INI-CET, or FMGE, here's why feeling behind doesn't mean you actually are.</p>

<h4>Everyone Shows Their Wins, Not Their Struggles</h4>

<p>Students post achievements. Good Grand Test scores. Completed revisions. Study hour screenshots.</p>

<p>What they don't post:</p>

<li>Mocks that went terrible</li>
<li>Chapters they haven't started</li>
<li>Days they didn't study</li>
<li>Topics they keep forgetting</li>

<p>You're comparing your entire journey with someone else's highlight reel. That creates fake pressure. You're only seeing a fraction of their actual prep.</p>

<h4>Your Prep Doesn't Have to Match Someone Else's</h4>

<p>Some people study six hours. Others manage ten. Some prep during internship. Others balance residency, work, or family stuff.</p>

<p>Different schedules don't mean different commitment.</p>

<p>The best prep is one you can actually stick to. Not the one that looks most impressive on Instagram.</p>

<h4>Progress Sneaks Up on You</h4>

<p>This part sucks about medical prep. Improvement feels invisible. For weeks you think you're forgetting everything. Then suddenly:</p>

<li>MCQs feel easier</li>
<li>Clinical cases make sense</li>
<li>You're faster at solving</li>
<li>Grand Test scores jump up</li>

<p>Learning happens slowly. Small gains pile up before you notice them.</p>

<h4>Revising What You Know Beats Starting New Topics</h4>

<p>Lots of aspirants panic because they haven't finished every subject. Instead of solidifying what they know, they jump to new topics. Result? Tons of reading. Almost no retention.</p>

<p>Someone who actually understands Medicine, Pharmacology, and Pathology will score higher than someone who rushed through everything once. Going deep matters more than covering everything.</p>

<h4>Mock Test Scores Aren't Your Final Rank</h4>

<p>A bad Grand Test score hurts. But these aren't predictions. They're roadmaps for what needs work.</p>

<p>Use them to figure out:</p>

<li>Which subjects need more revision</li>
<li>Where you make stupid mistakes</li>
<li>If time management is broken</li>

<p>Most students improve significantly in the final weeks because they actually learn from mocks.</p>

<h4>Panic Studying Doesn't Work</h4>

<p>When aspirants feel behind, they start studying 14-15 hours a day. Lasts three days. Then exhaustion hits.</p>

<p>Instead focus on:</p>

<li>Today's study plan</li>
<li>Yesterday's topics</li>
<li>Daily MCQ targets</li>
<li>Learning from tests</li>

<p>Small steady progress beats trying to do everything at once.</p>

<h4>Stop Comparing Your Timeline with Someone Else's</h4>

<p>Every aspirant starts differently. Some have strong basics. Some prepped after the internship. Some are balancing hospital shifts. Some are taking the exam for the second or third time.</p>

<p>Measure yourself against where you were last month. Not where someone else is today. That's the only comparison that matters.</p>

<h4>When You Feel Overwhelmed, Keep It Simple</h4>

<p>Ask yourself:</p>

<li>Did I study today?</li>
<li>Did I revise something I learned before?</li>
<li>Did I solve MCQs?</li>
<li>Did I understand at least one concept better than yesterday?</li>

<p>Answer yes to any of those? You're moving forward. That's progress.</p>

<h4>Final Thoughts</h4>

<p>NEET PG, INI-CET, FMGE prep isn't about keeping pace with everyone else. It's about steadily getting better at your own knowledge, confidence, exam skills.</p>

<p>Feeling behind happens. Especially when you're around other ambitious people. But feelings lie sometimes.</p>

<p>Stay consistent. Keep revising. Trust what you're building. Your rank won't depend on who seemed ahead during prep. It'll depend on what you actually know on exam day.</p>

<h4>FAQs</h4>

<p><b>Q: Is it normal to feel behind during NEET PG preparation?</b><br/>A: Yes, there are so many aspirants who have experienced this.</p>

<p><b>Q: Should I compare my preparation with others?</b><br/>A: No, you need to focus only on your own progress.</p>

<p><b>Q: Do mock test scores define my final rank?</b><br/>A: No, they help to identify your core improvement areas.</p>

<p><b>Q: Is revision more important than covering new topics?</b><br/>A: Yes, having a strong revision improves retention.</p>

<p><b>Q: How can I stay motivated during preparation?</b><br/>A: Stay consistent and track your daily progress for better understanding.</p>`,
