    para: `<p>NEET PG 2026 registration changed something major that's confusing a lot of candidates. The correspondence address.</p>

<p>Used to be: register early, get the center you want. Not anymore. System's completely different.</p>

<p>Your correspondence address is now critical for test center allocation. Get it wrong and you might end up somewhere inconvenient. Here's what you actually need to know before you submit.</p>

<h4>Test Centers Aren't First-Come, First-Served Anymore</h4>

<p>This is the biggest change. Registering on day one? Doesn't matter. Registering on the last day? Doesn't matter either.</p>

<p>When you register has zero impact on your test center allocation now. So stop rushing. Focus on getting the details right instead.</p>

<h4>How Test Center Selection Works Now</h4>

<p>Old system: you picked specific cities. New system: you pick states only.</p>

<p>You'll choose:</p>

<li>State Preference 1</li>
<li>State Preference 2</li>
<li>State Preference 3</li>

<p>That's it. No picking individual cities. Which is why your correspondence address matters way more now.</p>

<h4>Why Your Correspondence Address Actually Matters</h4>

<p>Your first state preference links directly to the state in your correspondence address. Basically, the address you put down should match where you're actually planning to be during the exam.</p>

<p>Real examples:</p>

<li>You're doing coaching in another state? Use that address if that's where you'll be.</li>
<li>Staying with relatives somewhere else before the exam? Put their address.</li>
<li>Right address means you're more likely to get a test center nearby.</li>

<h4>Don't Mess Up Your PIN Code</h4>

<p>This matters more than people realize. One wrong digit in your PIN code changes your entire location. Test centers are based on your correspondence address plus PIN code. Get it wrong and you end up somewhere inconvenient.</p>

<p>Before you submit:</p>

<li>Check every single digit of the PIN code</li>
<li>Verify the locality and state match</li>
<li>Make sure the address is where you'll actually be</li>

<p>One small mistake creates real problems.</p>

<h4>Picking Your State Preferences</h4>

<p>After your first state, you need two more. Think practically. Pick nearby states that make travel sense.</p>

<p>Examples:</p>

<li>First preference Kerala? Pick Tamil Nadu or Karnataka. They're close by.</li>
<li>Same logic everywhere. Choose states that are actually reachable.</li>

<p>If your first choice doesn't work out, you've still got somewhere accessible.</p>

<h4>Have Documents Ready</h4>

<p>Get proof of your correspondence address before you register. A document that matches that address.</p>

<p>Before you finish your application:</p>

<li>Your address is accurate</li>
<li>PIN code is correct</li>
<li>You have documents supporting that address</li>

<p>Keeps problems from popping up later.</p>

<h4>You Can't Change This Afterward</h4>

<p>This is important. Your correspondence address can't be edited during the edit window. Some details you can fix later. Not this one.</p>

<p>Everything matters here. Address, state, PIN code. Verify it all before you submit. Don't assume you'll fix it later. You absolutely cannot.</p>

<h4>Checklist Before You Submit</h4>

<p>Before you click submit:</p>

<li>Pick the state where you'll actually take the exam</li>
<li>Your correspondence address is completely correct</li>
<li>Every digit of the PIN code is right</li>
<li>Your second and third state preferences make sense</li>
<li>You have proof of your address</li>
<li>You've reviewed everything because you can't change it later</li>

<h4>Final Thoughts</h4>

<p>The correspondence address isn't just another form field anymore. New system means it actually matters.</p>

<p>Take an extra few minutes. Verify everything carefully. Get the address and PIN code right. This keeps you from getting stuck with a test center nowhere near where you'll be.</p>

<h4>FAQs</h4>

<p><b>Q: Is NEET PG 2026 test centre allotment first come, first served?</b><br/>A: No. When you register doesn't affect anything anymore.</p>

<p><b>Q: Can I choose my exam city?</b><br/>A: No. You pick three states. That's all you get.</p>

<p><b>Q: Why is the correspondence address important?</b><br/>A: It determines what test center you get.</p>

<p><b>Q: Can I edit my correspondence address later?</b><br/>A: Nope. Can't change it during the edit window.</p>

<p><b>Q: Should I verify my PIN code before submitting?</b><br/>A: Yeah. One wrong digit messes up your test center allocation.</p>`,
